%N A400916 Array A(n,k) read by antidiagonals: number of unit n-cubes added in the k-th shell by expanding an n-cube by one unit in every coordinate direction per step.
%C A400916 For each n >= 1, start with the unit n-cube. At each step, increase each side length by 2. A(n,k) is the number of unit n-cubes added in the k-th shell. Equivalently, A(n,k) is the number of points of Z^n having L_infinity norm k.
%H A400916 Eddie Lin Rui, <a href="/A400916/b400916.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10011; the first 141 antidiagonals of the array</a>
%F A400916 A(n,k) = (2*k+1)^n - (2*k-1)^n, for n >= 1, k >= 1.
%e A400916 The array begins:
%e A400916          k=1       2        3         4          5          6           7           8
%e A400916   n=1      2       2        2         2          2          2           2           2
%e A400916   n=2      8      16       24        32         40         48          56          64
%e A400916   n=3     26      98      218       386        602        866        1178        1538
%e A400916   n=4     80     544     1776      4160       8080      13920       22064       32896
%e A400916   n=5    242    2882    13682     42242     102002     210242      388082      660482
%e A400916   n=6    728   14896   102024    413792    1240120    3055248     6563816    12746944
%e A400916   n=7   2186   75938   745418   3959426   14704202   43261346   108110858   239479298
%e A400916   n=8   6560  384064  5374176  37281920  171312160  601371840  1747159904  4412866816
%e A400916 Using the array formula, for n = 3, k = 5: A(3,5) = (2*5+1)^3 - (2*5-1)^3 = 11^3 - 9^3 = 1331 - 729 = 602.
%o A400916 (Python)
%o A400916 def A(n,k): return (2*k+1)**n-(2*k-1)**n
%o A400916 for s in range(2,12):
%o A400916     print(*(A(n,s-n) for n in range(1,s)))
